Bluetooth for RISC OS - VERSION
===============================

What it does
  Plays the computer's sound through Bluetooth speakers and headphones
  (A2DP), and works with Bluetooth mice and keyboards, classic and Low
  Energy, through a USB Bluetooth adapter.

What you need
  RISC OS 5 on a Raspberry Pi (tested on a CM4 with RISC OS 5.31).
  Other RISC OS 5 machines with working USB 2 host drivers, such as a
  Pinebook Pro, should work with a USB adapter; on them the Pi USB
  drivers in the package are left alone.
  A USB Bluetooth adapter with a CSR8510 chip, such as the TP-Link UB400.
  SharedUnixLibrary 1.16 or later (a copy is included).

Installing
  1. Plug in the adapter.
  2. Double-click !Install and click Install.
  The application goes into !Boot.Resources, the settings plug-in into
  Configure, and two start-up entries are added: one loads the drivers
  before the desktop starts, one starts the icon bar task. Bluetooth is
  running as soon as Install finishes.

Using it
  Click the Bluetooth icon on the icon bar, put your device in pairing
  mode, click Scan, choose the device and click Connect. Paired devices
  come back by themselves after a restart; a sleeping mouse or keyboard
  comes back when it wakes. See !Help inside !Bluetooth for more.

Removing
  Double-click !Install and click Remove. Your pairings stay in
  Choices:Bluetooth in case you install again.

Known limits
  Audio uses SBC at a modest bitrate; quality depends on the radio link,
  so keep the adapter clear of the computer (a short USB extension lead
  helps). Headset microphones (HFP) are not supported in this version.
